  "notes": "The big-voice narrative ballad — the genre's benchmark for a slow-build story song with a full-throat final chorus. The session is the most completely documented in the section and the drum grammar comes straight out of it: a live kit tracked in a converted house with a \"Sky Hook — an SM57 mounted 14 feet above the kit and heavily compressed\" for the explosive chorus character, which is authored as a wide accent-to-main velocity spread and room-driven fills into the final chorus rather than as a mic (the engine has no signal chain). The stated grammar is \"cross-stick or hats verse, snare on 2&4 from the chorus, big room-mic-driven fills into the final chorus,\" and the bass is \"root motion with a walking pickup into each chorus; long notes under verses.\" Melody follows the entry literally: \"low, narrow, speech-like verse; chorus leaps a sixth or more and holds,\" so `intervals.leap` is raised against a step-dominant mix and the register spans from a spoken alto to a belted top. The final-chorus \"modal/plagal ♭VI or IV-of-IV lift\" is the ♭VI family. Tonic choice is documented as a rule, not a preference: \"the tonic chosen for a chorus that sits at the top of her range.\" ⚠ The signature record's ~117 reading is marked [LOW-CONF] in the research, as are the 66/64/141 anchors. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the countermelody ratio — \"orchestra bells sampled on a synth as the signature high colour\" is a timbre direction, and 0.48 / −2 dB are this author's reading of it.",
